<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> religious leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya">Salihiyya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order who founded the Dervish movement in the 1890s. For two decades his Dervish State fought British, Italian and Ethiopian forces in the Somali lands from forts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taleh">Taleh</a>. A British campaign using aircraft destroyed the state in 1920, and he died later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan.jpg" alt="Mohammed Abdullah Hassan" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Somali government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sayyid_Mohammed_Abdullah_Hassan.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mohammed Abdullah Hassan was born on 7 April 1856 in the Sacmadeeqa valley in the Ciid region, to a religious, pastoral family that traced its origins to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a> clan on his father's side and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhulbahante">Dhulbahante</a> clan on his mother's. He had memorised the entire Qur'an by the age of eleven and later studied at centres of learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>, Mogadishu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> under more than seventy teachers. In the early 1890s he completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, where he was influenced by the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya">Salihiyya order</a>, whose strict, reformist ideas shaped his militant outlook.</p><p>Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> in 1895, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a>, built a mosque and preached against the British colonial presence and Christian missionary activity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya">Salihiyya</a> clashed there with the established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a>, and in 1897 debates with Qadiriyya sheikhs, which the Qadiriyya reportedly won, left both sides accusing each other of heresy. The British banished him from Berbera.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1899 he founded the Dervish movement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya">Salihiyya</a> principles, partly as a rebuke to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a>'s accommodation of the colonists. He framed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> as a defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> sovereignty and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> against foreign Christian encroachment, appealing to clans frustrated by colonial taxes, land seizures and missionary activity. The conversion of Somali children by the French Catholic mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> in 1899 was a spark.</p><p>Towards the end of 1900 the Ethiopian emperor Menelik proposed joint action with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. At Jidaale on 9 January 1904 General Charles Egerton killed 1,000 Dervishes. Around 1909 about 400 followers left him on receiving a letter of excommunication from the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya">Salihiyya</a>, and in the same year the Dervish killed Sheikh Uways al-Barawi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a> rival. In 1909 and 1910 the Dervish capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taleh">Taleh</a>, where they built three stone forts. By 1913 they dominated much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> hinterland. At Dul Madoba on 9 August 1913 they killed Colonel Richard Corfield, who commanded the Somaliland Camel Constabulary, together with others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the beginning of 1920 the British struck the Dervish settlements with a coordinated air and land attack and defeated them, damaging their forts and inflicting great losses. Hassan fled westward into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a> and in October settled at Guano Imi in Arsi country with about four hundred followers. Hunger and sickness killed most of them. He died of influenza on 21 December 1920, and his grave is believed to lie near Imi, though its exact place is unknown.</p><p>The British called him the Mad Mullah, a pejorative; in 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> styled him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of the Somali People. Some regard him as an icon of Pan-Somalism and a father of Somali nationalism. He was also a poet, and several of his poems survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mohammed Abdullah (Sayyid)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1899 the Dervish State of <b>Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</b> fought a long war against the British, ended in 1920 by a campaign that used aircraft.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-somaliland">British Somaliland</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</b>, a scholar of the Salihiyya Sufi order, returned from Arabia in the mid-1890s and began preaching in Berbera against British rule and Christian missions.</blockquote><a href="/state/dervish-state">Dervish State</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Nur Ahmed Aman was among the leaders who opposed British and missionary influence and joined <b>Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</b> in founding the Dervish movement in the 1890s.</blockquote><a href="/state/habr-yunis-sultanate">Habr Yunis Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Imi, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.46,42.16&amp;year=1897" aria-label="Imi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5718 1922 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mohammed Abdullah Hassan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6168 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6168 2192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=6.46,42.16&amp;year=1897">What was here in 1897? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
